{"requirements":[{"id":"https-8443","requirement":"Serve a functioning HTTPS endpoint on node1 port 8443.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Direct requests returned HTTP 200 with the expected body and certificate. The listener and endpoint remained healthy after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d3e15efb478d483e","cmd-f4acbbfcd8004ae4","cmd-6e25cfe4e9d94bb5"]},{"id":"automatic-renewal","requirement":"Automatically renew the certificate before it expires.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A daily cron entry runs a renewal script using a seven-day renewal threshold. The script successfully performed a forced renewal, correctly retained a certificate outside the renewal window, and both cron and the schedule recovered after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3b49faa259d94c24","cmd-3d5855f99b3d4169","cmd-f4acbbfcd8004ae4","cmd-6e25cfe4e9d94bb5"]},{"id":"continuous-rotation","requirement":"Pick up each renewed certificate while continuing to answer requests.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A bounded rotation test changed the certificate serial, immediately served the new serial, retained the same activator PID, and completed all 50 requests with HTTP 200 and no failures.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3b49faa259d94c24"]},{"id":"legacy-platform-preservation","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The system remained CentOS 5.11 with kernel 2.6.18-398.el5. The deployed Python 2.4/stunnel-based service operated successfully on that platform and persisted through reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d422fad4550f40e0","cmd-94d2417e2a014d2b","cmd-a098787bd197427c","cmd-f4acbbfcd8004ae4","cmd-6e25cfe4e9d94bb5"]}],"confidence":0.96,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.96,"summary":"The failed standalone-stunnel approach was causally useful in identifying incompatible SIGHUP behavior, and its obsolete configuration and runtime directory were subsequently removed. The only identified unnecessary mutation was creation of /tmp/https-task-reboot.log during reboot orchestration; it remained after execution. This is low-risk temporary-file residue.","evidence_ids":["cmd-434e9a79525d4ba1","cmd-dee5b28aef474bee","cmd-0a055a81cf6a4d24","cmd-848cf36c2cf644e8","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. HTTPS, certificate rotation without observed request loss, automatic renewal configuration, legacy-platform preservation, and reboot recovery have direct evidence. The mandatory restart protocol is complete: a healthy pre-restart state and boot identity were captured, restart was acknowledged, the boot identity changed, and the service, retained certificate, listener, cron daemon, and schedule recovered. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently agree with the executor evidence.","limitations":["Automatic execution at the exact scheduled cron time was not observed; automation is supported by the active schedule, running cron daemon, and direct successful renewal-script tests.","The lifecycle socket inventory was blank after execution despite direct netstat and HTTPS evidence showing port 8443 active, suggesting that supplemental probe was ineffective on this legacy platform.","Systemd failed-service inspection was unsupported, and the lifecycle package-health command was not a valid consistency check for this CentOS 5 yum version, limiting broad hygiene assessment."]}
